# Hand-Drawn Art Style

The canonical look for this western platformer. Match it whenever creating or
replacing art. Approved reference images live in
`assets/source/style_reference/` (cowgirl on-foot, mounted, run strip) — pass
them, plus the relevant existing frame, as references when generating new art.
Approved concept sources for the ninja enemy live in `assets/source/ninja/`.

## Visual language

Warm **hand-painted storybook western cartoon**. Two tiers:

- **Tier A — chibi characters** (player, humanoid enemies/bosses): oversized
  friendly head ~40% of height, small chunky body, short stubby limbs, big
  boots/hat. Large expressive eyes, simple warm smile.
- **Tier B — naturalistic cartoon** (animals, terrain, structures, props,
  power-up icons): correct proportions, softer painterly shading, more internal
  detail (fur/scale/grain).

Shared finishing (both tiers):

- **Thick warm dark-brown ink outline** (`~(28,12,4)`, never pure black); thinner
  ink for internal seams.
- **Soft cel shading**: one base + one shadow + one highlight per material.
- **Light from upper-left**; soft low contact shadow when on ground.
- Warm desert palette: tan hat, denim-blue shirt/jeans, red kerchief, brown
  vest/boots, brass buckles; orange-red mesa rock; grass-green over brown dirt.

## Character pose conventions (match the cowboy)

- **idle** and **celebrate**: front-facing. Idle head/gaze angled slightly
  off-camera (3/4) — the character does **not** stare straight at the player.
- **run** (4-frame cycle) and **jump**: 3/4 view **facing right** (the engine
  flips for left movement).
- **Idle is a single pose frame** (idle_0 == idle_1) — never alternate mismatched
  idle frames (causes flicker).
- **Enemies that always face the player** (e.g. the ninja) use a 3/4 **facing-right**
  idle/ready stance instead of a front idle, so it blends when the run cycle
  interleaves the idle frame. Keep weapons in action frames **compact** (blade tip
  close to the body) so the head-to-foot body scale stays consistent across frames.

## Generation + integration pipeline

1. Generate with the image tool, passing `assets/source/style_reference/*` and the
   existing frame as references. Ask for a plain flat background.
2. Generators paint a **fake light checkerboard / white background** (not real
   alpha). Key it out with `tools/art_pipeline.py::cutout` (border flood-fill of
   light near-gray pixels; preserves interior eye-whites).
3. Frame with `art_pipeline.frame_sprite`:
   - **On-foot player frames**: 64×64, figure ~60 px tall, feet on y=63, centered.
   - **Mounted frames**: `ride_*` 360×320 (fill ~280 px, ~20 px top/bottom margin);
     `jump` 336×268 (hooves at bottom).
4. Generate a **run cycle as one horizontal 4-frame strip** (keeps the character
   consistent), then split with `art_pipeline.slice_strip`.
5. **Climb** is a **2-frame strip** (`climb_0` / `climb_1`) for cowboy and cowgirl;
   rebuild with `python tools/build_ladder_art.py`.
6. **`_boots` (Magic Boots) variants are identical copies** of the base frame —
   the boots glow is a separate in-engine overlay, not baked into the sprite.
7. After changing shipped art, bump `content_version.txt`.

Cowgirl on-foot frames live in `assets/player/cowgirl/`; mounted in
`assets/world/cowgirl_horse_*.png`. Cowboy frames in `assets/player/` are the
Tier-A reference for pose, scale, and in-frame registration.

Ninja enemy frames live in `assets/world/ninja_*.png` (idle, run_0/1, sword_0/1,
throw_0/1, jump_0/1, tied on a **64×80** canvas — body ~67 px, feet on y=76 — plus the
28×28 `ninja_shuriken` projectile). Rebuild them from `assets/source/ninja/` with
`python tools/build_ninja_frames.py`. Enemy action animations are authored as
**2-frame horizontal strips** (run / sword / throw / jump).

Outlaw Kingpin walk frames live in `assets/world/boss_outlaw_kingpin_walk_*.png`
(130×180, matching standing `boss_outlaw_kingpin.png`). Rebuild from
`assets/source/kingpin/walk_strip.png` with `python tools/build_kingpin_frames.py`.
The four frames share one scale, the standing art's foot row, and an upper-body
sway anchor, so the drawn head bob survives, the boots stay on one ground line,
and the torso does not slide as the stride opens. Do not re-normalise each frame
to its own height — that flattens the bob and pops his size against the standing
pose.

Stampede Bull boss frames live in `assets/world/boss_stampede_bull.png` and
`boss_stampede_bull_run_*.png` on a **440×230** canvas (standing body ~164 px,
feet on y=222). Rebuild from `assets/source/bull/run_strip_boss.png` (and reframe the
standing stun art) with `python tools/build_bull_frames.py`. The packed strip
overlaps neighbouring bulls in x, so do **not** hard-cut at density valleys —
flood from each density peak while excluding neighbouring cores, or horns and
tails get sliced off. Normalize each run pose to the standing frame's **opaque
painted area** (within 4%), not just canvas or bounding-box height; tucked poses
otherwise look much smaller. Keep clear side margin (≥40 px standing, ≥60 px run).

The treasure chest uses full-frame closed/open sprites (`treasure_chest_closed`
~66×48, `treasure_chest_open` same width / taller, `treasure_chest_stamp` 64×64).
Rebuild from `assets/source/chest/` (`closed.png` / `open.png`) with
`python tools/build_chest_frames.py`. Do **not** slice a hinged lid/body/interior
rig — the open state is the painted `open.png`. Use **dark espresso wood + bright gold**
so it keeps strong value/accent contrast against the warm orange-red mesa
backdrops (a mid-brown chest blends in). Both frames keep the painted base on the
walk surface (bottom-aligned feet).

Workshop / trail-editor kid icons live in `assets/ui/menu_icon_*.png` (back, edit,
add, remove, restore, export, import, add_trail, save, play, scroll_left/right,
plus shared hat/heart/hammer/gear). Rebuild with `python tools/build_workshop_icons.py`.
Keep the simple western silhouette style so kids can read buttons without text.
